sri lanka's defense minister says police  have arrested seven suspects believed to  be linked to the wave of bombings that  killed at least 190 people  the first seven blasts targeted churches  and luxury hotels  in and near the capital colombo a church  in the eastern town of batikalau was  also hit  one of the suspects was arrested on the  outskirts of colombo  at the scene of an eighth explosion that  killed three police  as they attempted to search a house most  of the casualties came from the church  bombings where worshipers were  celebrating easter  the prime minister of sri lanka ranil  wicker messenger has been speaking about  the attacks today  let's have a listen  i would like to express my condolences  with the families of victims  and those injured in the attacks that  took place in the country today  i see this as a major crisis that will  lead the country  and its economy towards instability  i strongly condemn these attacks that  targeted religious  sites and luxury hotels  all of us must uphold the law of the  land  let's cross over now to jamila najmudin  editor of news in asia and journalists  in colombo sri lanka  uh at least two more blasts followed the  earlier bombings today what's the  situation in  and around colombo now  yes hello so basically the latest  information  is that the security forces have  arrested  seven people in connection with the  eight blasts  which took place today and uh security  also has been beefed up  across the island especially in the  places of worship  and an island-wide curfew has been  imposed until further northeast  um do we have any information yet on the  suspects arrested  uh we do not have any information as to  which  organization these suspects belong to  because the police are not divulging  much to us  but we do know that two of the suspects  are currently  trapped in a house in the matagorda  where one of the explosions took place  and the police have surrounded the area  officials have imposed a social media  ban in sri lanka following the bombings  what's the aim of that so basically when  the  uh attacks to explosions took place  today morning  there was a lot of misinformation which  started coming out on social  media which expressed panic and fear  amongst the public  it was also igniting racism so just a  short while ago president michael farmer  citizen released a statement  urging people not to release false  information  which would create further pension in  the country  so the aim of this is basically to  spread misinformation we are traveling  out  this is of course an ongoing operation  you talked about panic in the streets  what's the situation  now well basically the streets are  deserted at the moment since there's a  curfew in place  until further notice and those who wish  to travel to the airport who have flight  are us to show their tickets in their  passports at all the security  checkpoints  so you do not see anybody on the roads  at the moment except for the triforces  um you talked about the curfew what  other measures are officials  implementing at the moment  so basically uh the police have been  dispatched outside all places of worship  across the island and also outside all  these star class hotels in colombo  and the rest of the country and there's  a search and rescue operation going on  by  by the authorities in all the explosion  sites  that is the only information which we  are hearing at the moment  sri lanka's christian community was hit  particularly hard by these bombings  during his easter mass in the vatican  pope francis  condemned the violence let's listen  i heard with sadness the news of the  horrendous attacks  which on easter sunday have brought  sadness  and pain to some churches  and other places in sri lanka  i would like to express my heartfelt  closeness  to the christian community  which was attacked while gathered in  prayer  and to all victims of such a cruel act  of violence  i entrust to the lord all who so  tragically died  and i pray for the wounded and all those  who are suffering because of this  dramatic event  let's get back to jamila najmudin  covering the story on the ground  uh jamila um we've heard about  explosions at  churches at hotels in residential areas  did this attack have a clear target  well initially when the attacks did  happen on sunday morning  it took place in three very popular  churches across the island  that is one church with an anthony's  church in capital colombo  uh the san sebastian's church in negambo  just outside the capital  and the zeon church in basically in the  eastern forest so initially what  everybody assumed was that  it was the catholic community which was  sought after during these attacks  but just within a short span of time  then there were also multiple attacks in  star-class hotels in colombo  where foreigners were also targeted uh  so the exact motive behind these attacks  are yet to be revealed  this is something that we already know  the worst violence in sri lanka since  the end of the civil war ten years ago  what can you tell us about the threats  facing the country at the moment  well basically um when the war ended in  2009  uh we uh we were getting back to normal  this is the 10th year since the end of  the war uh  in fact in may we will be celebrating 10  years  since the war ended um we were getting  back on track uh where the security  level was also  it was also minimized to a great extent  uh but with these attacks today uh  security has been leased up to the  maximum and it is likely to stay  like that at least for the coming weeks  only seven percent of sri lanka's  population is christian  they were of course at the center of  these attacks even if they weren't the  only target  have there been other attacks against  their this religious minority  in the past briefly if you can  yes there has been attacks against the  minorities in the past for example on  palm sunday last week  there was an attack at the methodist  church in anuradhapura which is  in the central coming and then  just a few months ago there were also  attacks in beginner against the minority  muslims  but the government was successful in uh  providing uh  uh safeguarding these minorities  so this attack comes as a total surprise  to all the minorities as well  jamila najmudin thank you very much  thank you a short while ago i spoke to  asanga  abeyaguna sekira head of the institute  of national security studies in colombo  sri lanka  he was in one of the hotels in sri  lanka's capital  that were targeted in today's attacks he  told us about what he witnessed  it was in the shangri-la hotel and so  there was at 8 30  5 the first uh i mean the initial blast  went off and then  i was on my way down to the breakfast  area so that's where it happened but  so the table won the breakfast area  where all the people were  having breakfast and most of them  celebrating the easter  and um so there was a lot of foreigners  and  there's so many victims from that  and i witnessed uh most of them and  actually the military barack said that  the military defense  the headquarters was right next to the  shangri-la hotel and you could see uh  amazingly  there was a lot of assistance coming  from the military and who was running in  while we were walking out of the  building they were mentally running into  assistant  and so many casualties so um  in there and uh there were  simultaneously uh there was uh  uh bombs from uh like everywhere like  seven seven other bombs from uh  uh in different different places in uh  sri lanka  and um from 2017 in march we've been uh  uh we'll be sort of highlighting the uh  our institute has been uh  national secretaries have been  highlighting this there is a serious  threat on the  violent extremist uh groups uh there are  there are so many  groups who are uh involved in sri lanka  and there could be a threat  uh serious significant sector coming  into sri lanka  and um and this sort of um i mean uh if  you take the uh  the targeted attacks in the san antonio  one of the uh most popular churches were  for the christians to come even from the  villages they  come in there and you could see many  victims uh  from that church attack and many other  uh churches  that was asanga abeyaguna sekira head of  the institute of national security  studies in colombo sri lanka he spoke to  us earlier by phone  let me quickly bring you up to speed on  the story we've been following for you  this hour  officials now say at least 207 people  have been killed and hundreds injured  in explosions at churches and hotels in  sri lanka  the first seven explosions hit colombo  as well as the town of negombo  and batikaloa in the east of the country  a major church  in colombo was among the buildings hit  other targets were five-star hotels  the defense minister says seven suspects  have been arrested  for more analysis let's bring in dw  sonya falnikar  in our studio in delhi sonia people  around the world are following events in  sri lanka what's been the reaction so  far in india and other countries in the  region  so well there's really been um a flood  of statements here across the asian  subcontinent  uh across the indian subcontinent really  condemning these attacks now here in  india of course which is a neighbor of  sri lanka's and a crucial ally  uh prime minister narendra modi has you  know issued a press release  he's also tweeted saying there is no  place for barbarism in our region  he he says he strongly condemns the  blast he said india stands in solidarity  with sri lanka  india's foreign minister sushma swaraj  says you know she's been in touch with  the indian high commission  in colombo she says she's monitoring the  situation very closely  her ministry has also tweeted helplines  for indian nationals  who are trapped in sri lanka  this incident recalls other attacks  we've seen in recent years in the region  in 2016 75 people were killed in an  easter sunday bombing targeting  christians in lahore pakistan  what parallels are there between these  incidents  well i have to say that so far really no  group has really claimed responsibility  for these attacks  in sri lanka and so i think we know very  little at this point about  who was really behind that what the  motive was um in terms of parallels i  mean you know the  the indian subcontinent is really no  stranger to to terror attacks  closer home in india if you remember  back in mumbai in 2008 we had  a terrorist attack but in you know  across  multiple sites in the financial capital  but but as far as parallels go i think  um we can say that you know all these  attacks have been very unexpected in  that they have taken authorities by  surprise  uh they have seemed to be very well  coordinated and well planned that was  the case in mumbai too  uh these attacks have also claimed very  a very high debt toll in mumbai if you  remember  in 2008 174 people died  so yeah there are certainly uh parallels  in that sense but i think if you look at  sri lanka in the context there we have  to remember that there has been really a  major  you know a lull in major violence really  since the end of the civil war in sri  lanka in 2009  now there has been some sporadic  violence between members  of the majority buddhist sinali's  community and  the muslim minority in sri lanka so much  so that last year last march sri lanka  did  you know impose um an emergency um  because of clashes between between the  the buddhist minority  but i think it's really safe to say that  since the end of the civil war since the  defeat of the tamil tigers in 2009  we really haven't seen an incident of  this kind in sri lanka  sonia falnikar thank you for your  analysis

